    - "Header content, nav links, or the docs sidebar rendered inside a centered max-width column on viewports wider than 1440px — producing bare background bleeding beside the chrome, a brand cluster floating adrift from the viewport-left edge, or a sidebar detached from the viewport-left edge and drifting inward with the centered grid. The terminal-frame-centered pattern applies to main content only; chrome and sidebar must hug the viewport."
    - "OS-native scrollbars rendering on any scroll container — specifically the docs sidebar `<aside>` where the default light-grey Chromium scrollbar clashes with the warm-dark palette, but also body scroll, code-block horizontal overflow, and any future overflow region. Every scroll container must ship the custom warm-dark scrollbar treatment; a native scrollbar anywhere is a spec violation."
    - A hardcoded list of doc pages anywhere in the codebase — if someone adds a markdown file under `.aide/docs/`, `.claude/commands/`, `.claude/agents/`, `.claude/skills/`, `.aide/reference/tools/`, or `.aide/reference/hooks/` and rebuilds, that page must appear in the docs nav and be routable without further edits.
    - Shipping the docs site with any of the six source categories missing or collapsed together — methodology, commands, agents, skills, tools, and hooks must each have their own dedicated top-level docs section.
    - The mascot being called "AIDEY" or "AID" anywhere in shipped output — copy, alt text, filenames under `public/`, component identifiers, tooltip strings, or any string a user or search engine could see — because those names undermine the Domain Expert framing and leak abandoned naming into shipped output.
    - "Shipping `tweaks.jsx`, any of the rejected variants (`variant-editorial.jsx`, `variant-linear.jsx`, `design-canvas.jsx`, `index.html`), or any other design-tool scaffolding: only the Terminal direction belongs in production."
    - Any server-side runtime, API route, middleware, ISR, or runtime Node dependency that prevents `next build` from producing a fully static export deployable to GitHub Pages.
    - Brand voice that reads as salesy, vague, or hype-driven — the audience is founders, operators, and technical leaders, and the site must sound precise, deliberate, and engineering-credible at every copy surface. Comparison pages carry no invented facts and no competitor number without an 'as of' date.
    - Broken or silently failing interactions — a pipeline stage that does not expand on click, a tree folder caret that does not rotate, a vault tab that does not swap content, a CLI demo that does not loop, a copy button that does not provide "✓ copied" feedback, or a mascot tooltip that never rotates with scroll position.
    - Search signals faked or wrong — structured data carrying fabricated values (ratings, dates, authors) or text the page does not show, a page whose share preview or structured data names another page, a second or contradicting site identity record, a build-time date anywhere a true last-updated date belongs, a dead security contact, or a crawler policy that shuts out search engines or AI crawlers. Structured data shipped as hygiene is fine; inventing what it says is not.
    - Cumulative layout shift on the hero as the typewriter headline types in — the space for the animated headline must be reserved before first paint.
    - Nav links to destinations that do not exist (the handoff's legacy `pipeline` link), a nav that differs from page to page, or outbound `github`/`npm` links that point anywhere other than `https://github.com/aidemd-mcp/server` and `https://www.npmjs.com/package/@aidemd-mcp/server`.
---

## Context

aidemd.dev is the public home of a methodology — AIDE (Autonomous Intent-Driven Engineering) — and of the package that ships it, `@aidemd-mcp/server`. The owner is Lowmeadow LLC, positioning itself as a thought leader at the intersection of agentic engineering, data pipelines, and practical AI for small and mid-market businesses. The audience is not consumers and not developers casually Googling a tool name: it is founders, operators, and technical leaders evaluating whether AIDE is a credible approach to shipping software with AI agents. Everything on the site filters through that audience's skepticism. Hype reads as amateur to them; precision reads as authority.

The site has three jobs, and they are distinct. Job one is to be the canonical public face of AIDE the methodology — a marketing landing page that communicates, within a single scroll, that AIDE treats a short intent spec next to the code as the contract every agent works from. Job two is to be the canonical reference for `@aidemd-mcp/server` — a docs area that renders the methodology docs, commands, agents, skills, and the tools and hooks reference that ship with the package. Job three is to be found and quoted: people asking Google, ChatGPT, Perplexity or Claude about intent-driven engineering should land on, and see cited, this site. A /learn section of definition, comparison and how-to pages carries that job, and every page — landing, docs, /learn, feature pages — must describe itself truthfully to the machines that read it. All three jobs are served by a single static export.

The docs render directly from this repository's filesystem on purpose. The package and the docs cannot drift, because the docs are literally the same markdown files the package ships. A reader on aidemd.dev and an agent reading the package after `npx @aidemd-mcp/server init` are looking at the same canonical text. This is a structural guarantee, not a workflow promise.

The visual execution is Terminal: warm-dark backgrounds, muted forest-green accents, JetBrains Mono for every character on the page. The audience builds in terminals; the aesthetic is a credibility signal before any word is read. The mascot, Expo, carries both the visual warmth and the domain-expert framing — AIDE is the AI Domain Expert methodology, and Expo is its personification on the site, guiding the visitor through the methodology as the resident expert.

## Strategy

**Ship as a static Next.js 14 App Router export with Tailwind and MDX.** Use `output: 'export'` and target GitHub Pages. The site is a docs+marketing surface with no per-user state and no dynamic data, so static generation is the smallest deploy surface available and the cheapest thing to keep up. Tailwind gives utility-first styling that keeps CSS bloat minimal and lets the warm-dark palette live in a single theme definition. MDX is the native format for markdown-sourced docs and for marketing copy that sometimes needs to embed a React component. No API routes, no ISR, no middleware, no runtime Node — any of those would break static export and move us off GitHub Pages.

**Drive the docs page list from a build-time filesystem walk across six content roots — `.aide/docs/`, `.claude/commands/`, `.claude/agents/`, `.claude/skills/`, `.aide/reference/tools/`, `.aide/reference/hooks/` — with zero exclusions.** Every `.md` file under those roots becomes a route. The full set is discovered via `generateStaticParams` at build time. A hardcoded list anywhere in the codebase is a spec violation — that rule is load-bearing against the drift-resistance guarantee in the frontmatter.

**Render the docs with one template that matches the docs-* design references exactly.** Breadcrumb, H1, DocMeta card, body, prev/next, footer — one component tree, driven by MDX frontmatter. Every docs route, regardless of which of the six categories it belongs to, goes through this single template. Uniformity is the credibility signal: the methodology docs and the subagent docs must feel like chapters of the same book.

**Keep the top-level nav to five entries, identical on every page, and expose the six docs sections inside the docs surface.** The nav is `docs`, `brain`, `observer`, `github ↗`, `npm ↗`; the handoff's legacy `pipeline` link points at nothing and does not ship. GitHub doubles as the v1 contact channel, and /learn sits in the footer. The sidebar orders methodology, commands, agents, skills, tools, hooks — the method, what users invoke, what the orchestrator delegates to, what agents load, what the server exposes, what it listens to; collapsing them understates the package.

**Treat each handoff-derived route's own design handoff as its visual source of truth; diff production output against it at that handoff's design widths.** The landing and docs diff against the Terminal JSX prototype files at 1440px; /brain against the brain handoff at its handoff's widths; /observer against its Variant A artboards at 1280 and 390. /learn has no handoff: it is assembled only from the site chrome and docs prose styles and checked for consistency with them, so it can never introduce a style of its own. The Terminal handoff README describes the visual system at the level of rules; the JSX files encode the pixel-level truth. When the README and a JSX file disagree, the JSX file wins. Named CSS variables mirror the handoff's `--bg`, `--card`, `--card-2`, `--fg`, `--dim`, `--dim-2`, `--border`, `--hover`, `--accent`, `--todo`, `--plan`, `--danger`. Typography is JetBrains Mono only, weights 400/500/600/700 — introducing a second typeface is a spec violation.

**Forest green is the only accent: `#3d6b4a` for fills, `#6aaa7c` wherever the green is text or an icon. No other green in the site palette.** Specifically: no pure green, no saturated green, no `#4caf50`-style material greens. The accent shows up as the left-border on emphasis cards (2px), as the `##` glyph on H2, as keyword color in syntax highlighting, as the active-state color on nav, and as the "canonical" footer dot. Page-scoped palettes are the exception: /observer's state colours (amber `#d6b25a`, danger `#ff5f56`, greys `#948e82`) render only on that page.

**Callouts and spacing each have one fixed scale.** Callouts use the 3px left border — NOTE accent-green `#3d6b4a`, WARN amber `#d6b25a`, INFO blue `#7aa6d6` — and no other callout style exists on the site. Spacing uses 4/8/12/14/16/18/20/24/28/32/40/44/60/64/80 px and nothing else. Ad-hoc values fail design review; the fixed scales are what make the site feel deliberate rather than thrown together.

**Name the mascot Expo everywhere — component, filenames, alt text, speech bubbles, on-page copy.** Rename the four handoff PNGs on copy to `public/`: `expo-teach.png`, `expo-think.png`, `expo-wave.png`, `expo-point.png`. Component name is `Expo`. No string visible to a user, screen reader, or search engine says "AIDEY" or the interim name "AID". The rationale is positioning: AIDE is a double entendre — Autonomous Intent-Driven Engineering and AI Domain Expert — and Expo personifies the Domain Expert half, reinforcing the methodology's positioning as the authoritative, expert-guided reference rather than its pronunciation.

**Drive mascot tips from IntersectionObserver watching section elements.** As the user scrolls, the nearest visible section sets the mascot's tip string. Tip copy is sourced from the handoff's `aidey.jsx` tip list, with "AIDEY" strings rewritten to "Expo". The mascot never blocks content, never auto-opens on load, and never reads like a chatbot.

**Default to server components with local state — no global store, no auth, no routing state beyond Next defaults.** Only surfaces that need state are client components: pipeline stage selector, intent-tree expand/select, vault-brain tabs, CLI typewriter, code-block and cite-URL copy, the Expo mascot; each owns its own state. Headlines, prose, docs and /learn bodies stay server-rendered: a smaller JS payload, a better Lighthouse score, and static HTML that AI crawlers — which do not run JavaScript — can read.

**Brand voice is precise, deliberate, engineering-credible. Every copy surface passes the "would a skeptical technical lead roll their eyes at this?" test.** Concrete claims, not marketing adjectives. "Treats a short intent spec as the contract every agent works from" passes; "Revolutionize your AI workflow" fails. No exclamation points in marketing copy. No words like "seamless", "cutting-edge", "game-changing". If a sentence could appear in any AI tool's landing page, rewrite it.

**Cite-as URLs follow the pattern `https://aidemd.dev/docs/<slug>?v=<commit>`, with `<commit>` injected at build time from `git log -1 --format=%h -- <file>` for each MDX file.** The `?v=<commit>` parameter is non-negotiable: every docs URL is permanently citable against a specific revision of the methodology. This is a thought-leadership credibility move — it says the text is a versioned artifact, not a document that might silently change tomorrow.

**Treat the "canonical" footer dot as an always-on authority signal, not a build-status indicator.** The dot is present, green, static. It tags this site — as owned and copyrighted by Lowmeadow LLC — as the real and authoritative source of AIDE. It does not pulse, does not respond to CI, does not link anywhere.

**Use GA4 with a production measurement ID, loaded via `next/script` strategy `afterInteractive`.** This overrides the handoff README's Plausible recommendation, by owner decision. `afterInteractive` protects the hero from CLS-sensitive paint timing. Respect standard do-not-track behavior.

**Syntax-highlight code blocks via Shiki or Prism with a custom theme keyed off the handoff accent colors.** Keywords `#3d6b4a`, strings `#d6b25a`, identifiers `#e8dfce`, against the `#0a0906` warm-dark code interior. Pre-render highlighting at build time if possible, to keep JS payload small.

**Reserve the hero's typewriter headline space before first paint, so CLS is zero on the hero.** The animated headline area has fixed dimensions computed from the finished text; the typewriter reveals characters inside that reserved box.

**Target evergreen browsers for v1 — last two versions of Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge.** Older-browser compatibility is a follow-up review. The ≥95 Lighthouse gate in all four categories runs at 1440px desktop on the home page, a doc page and a /learn page, because each is built from a different template and one passing proves nothing about the others. Mobile (375px) is best-effort: responsive down, sidebar collapses to a drawer below 768px, no strict gate.

**One /learn roster and one true last-updated date per page feed every machine-readable surface.** The docs registry and the /learn roster are the only page lists; the sitemap, llms.txt, llms-full.txt, markdown copies and Atom feed read them at build, so a new page reaches every surface at once. Each date comes from its source's version history and shows identically on the page, in the sitemap and in structured data. Google trusts lastmod only when consistently accurate, so a missing date is omitted, never build-stamped.

**Build every page's share preview through one metadata helper fed by that page's own record.** Next merges metadata shallowly, so a page that writes its own preview silently loses the shared URL, image and site name with no build error. One helper restating every field on every page, plus a per-page test on the preview's URL and image, turns that into a failed build. Title, description, canonical URL and image come from one record, so preview and page cannot name different pages.

**Keep one site identity record — Lowmeadow LLC as the organization, the website, logo, real profiles — on the home page; every other page references it by id.** Google reads the website entity only on a domain's home page, and each inline restatement creates a second, unlinked entity that drifts. Lowmeadow LLC is the author and publisher of every docs and /learn page; there is no person node. Its only profiles are the AIDE GitHub organization and npm package — the company has no website or company profile of its own to link, and profiles that do not exist are left out, never guessed.

**Win citations on the visible page; ship structured data as hygiene, built from the same data as the copy.** Answer-engine fetchers read visible HTML and ignore JSON-LD, and pages that added schema saw no citation lift. The measured levers are visible: headings matching the searched phrase (41% vs 30% cited), the answer in the first third, 120–180-word sections, and AIDE paired with 'intent-driven' or 'aidemd' since bare AIDE names other projects. Breadcrumb, FAQ and definition markup derive from the visible text, never inventing values.

**Crawler policy: search yes, AI input yes, AI training yes; Bytespider blocked.** Answer bots are separate tokens from training bots, and a site that turns them away drops out of their answers, so both are welcomed explicitly. Training is allowed so models learn AIDE as the intent-driven engineering reference. llms.txt and its full-text sibling ship for coding agents, their one confirmed reader, not as a citation lever.

**Ship /learn drafts live and indexed; the founder reviews them in place.** A held-back page earns no crawl, index or citation time, and a wording fix lands in that page's one copy record, which moves its true date everywhere at once. A draft's real risk is a false fact, not rough wording, so comparison pages carry no invented facts and every competitor number shows an 'as of' date.

**Anticipate a sub-spec tree.** Sub-specs narrow this root for the marketing sections, the docs shell and template, the docs registry, the Expo mascot, the /brain guide, the /observer page, the /learn roster, template and pages, and the llms.txt surfaces. Handoff-derived modules are pixel-checked against their own handoff, /learn against the site chrome. The search plumbing every page shares — metadata helper, identity record, sitemap, crawler policy — stays at root scope. This spec names the constraints; the sub-specs name the shapes.

## Good examples

A correctly-rendered marketing hero text surface:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init

Your intent is the contract every agent works from.
```

The prompt is dim, the command is in JetBrains Mono weight 500, the headline is 36px weight 600, letter-spacing `-0.5px`. The headline area is pre-reserved so no layout shifts as the typewriter runs.

A correctly-rendered docs page header for `aide-spec.mdx`:

```
AIDE / DOCS

AIDE Spec

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Published     2026-04-13T13:47:06-07:00                     │
│ Source commit b859854                                        │
│ Cite as       https://aidemd.dev/docs/aide-spec?v=b859854  ⎘ │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

Breadcrumb is uppercase, small, dim. H1 is 36px weight 600. DocMeta is a 2-column grid inside a card with `1px` border and `6px` radius. The cite-as value has a dotted green underline, click-to-copy, swaps to `✓ copied` for 1500ms.

A correct top-level nav row, left to right, JetBrains Mono, aligned right of the window chrome:

```
docs   brain   observer   github ↗   npm ↗
```

Five entries. In that order, on every page. Active page gets an accent-green underline. Commands, agents, skills, tools, and hooks appear in the docs sidebar, not here.

A correct Expo speech bubble tip as the reader scrolls past the pipeline section:

> Expo says: the pipeline is six commands. Each one reads the spec and writes to the spec.

The "AIDEY" string is never present. The bubble is pinned to the mascot, 58px wide mascot image, bottom-right fixed.

A correctly-rendered NOTE callout inside a docs page:

```
┃ NOTE
┃
┃ Every `.aide` file must have a scope, description, intent, and
┃ outcomes block. The QA agent treats missing frontmatter as a
┃ spec violation.
```

Left border is 3px solid `#3d6b4a`. The `NOTE` label is uppercase, 11px, weight 600, accent-green. Body text is 14px weight 400, `#e8dfce`, line-height `1.75`.

A correct /learn definition page: the H1 uses the searched phrase ("Intent-driven engineering"), the first paragraph opens with the definition, each H2 section runs 120–180 words, and the visible updated date is the same date the sitemap and the page's structured data carry. The byline names Lowmeadow LLC, and the page's structured data references the home page's identity record by id as author and publisher, with no person node and no restated organization.

## Bad examples

A nav row that reads `docs   pipeline   brain   github ↗   npm ↗`. This is the handoff's legacy nav: `pipeline` points at nothing shippable and `observer` is missing. Failing this shows an audience of technical leaders that the site can't even keep its navigation honest.

A mascot tooltip that says `AIDEY says: welcome to aidemd.dev`. The name is Expo, not AIDEY. The Domain Expert framing is the whole reason the mascot exists; shipping "AIDEY" (or the interim "AID") undoes it.

A `/docs` index page that lists only the methodology docs and omits the command, agent, skill, tool, and hook docs. This is the hardcoded-list failure mode — the filesystem walk either misses some of the six roots or a static array somewhere bypasses it. Adding a new markdown file and rebuilding must produce a new route automatically.

A hero where the typewriter reveals one character at a time and the headline row visibly grows taller as characters fill the line, pushing the content below down several pixels. This is the CLS failure the frontmatter explicitly forbids. The headline's final box must be reserved before first paint.

A code block whose keyword color is `#4caf50` — a material-design green — where `#3d6b4a` belongs. This violates the "muted forest green, never pure or saturated" rule and is recognizable on sight as an off-palette mistake.

A docs page whose DocMeta "Cite as" row reads `https://aidemd.dev/docs/aide-spec` with no `?v=<commit>` suffix. This breaks the permanent-citability promise — the URL drifts silently if the underlying markdown changes. Every cite-as URL must carry the commit hash.

A marketing section whose headline reads `Revolutionize your AI-powered engineering workflow today!`. Exclamation point, generic adjectives ("revolutionize", "AI-powered"), filler word ("today"). A skeptical technical lead reads this and closes the tab. The replacement is concrete: `Your intent is the contract every agent works from.`

A callout that uses a 2px left border and a muted grey label, not the 3px accent-colored border and uppercase label the handoff specifies. Subtle, and exactly the kind of "close but not exact" deviation the pixel-for-pixel outcome forbids.

A sitemap where every page's last-modified date is the day of the last build, while the doc page itself shows a date from months earlier. The build passes; search engines learn the field is noise and stop trusting it, and the page tells two stories about its own age.

A doc page that sets its own share title and description by hand. It renders and builds fine, but its preview loses the shared URL and image, so a link pasted into Slack shows the home page's image and URL. The page names another page — exactly the "faked or wrong" search signal the frontmatter forbids.

A crawler policy that blocks every AI bot "to protect the methodology". The answer bots are shut out along with everything else, and the site disappears from the ChatGPT and Perplexity answers it exists to be cited in.

